---
// The money page (WEBSITE.md §5.5), in the posture the maintainer chose in
// session (#116, 2026-08-20): INTENT PLUS DIRECT CLUB LINKS. OurHike takes
// no money today - the structure for money to follow the trail it came from
// is designed (features/PRICING_MODEL.md) and nothing is charged before it
// is built. What this page must make unmistakable either way is the
// differentiator: where money goes, and that nothing safety-relevant is
// ever behind it.
//
// The giving links point at the organisations' own front doors rather than
// deep links to donation forms - their sites move, ours should not claim to
// know their layout, and both put giving front and centre themselves.
import Base from '../layouts/Base.astro'

const title = 'Support the trail — OurHike'
const description =
  'How OurHike is funded, the two commitments that hold either way, and how to give directly to the organizations that maintain these trails today.'
---

<Base title={title} description={description}>
  <main class="wrap">
    <header class="section">
      <div class="section__head">
        <span class="blaze"></span>
        <h2>Where the money goes</h2>
      </div>
      <p class="lede">
        OurHike doesn't take donations yet — and that is a decision, not an
        oversight. A funding model where money follows the trail it came from
        is designed and public; we won't take a dollar before the structure
        that honours it is built. Two commitments hold either way:
      </p>
    </header>

    <section class="section--tight">
      <div class="cards" style="max-width: 980px">
        <div class="card">
          <h3>Nothing safety-relevant is ever behind a paywall.</h3>
          <p>
            Water, closures, hazards, and the map itself stay free for
            everyone, always. If paid tiers ever exist they cover convenience,
            never safety — staying safe on the trail must not require a
            subscription. That is the point of the project, not a promotion.
          </p>
        </div>
        <div class="card">
          <h3>The trail needs money today — give it directly.</h3>
          <p>
            The
            <a href="https://appalachiantrail.org/">Appalachian Trail
            Conservancy</a>, clubs like the
            <a href="https://www.nynjtc.org/">New York–New Jersey Trail
            Conference</a>, and preserves like
            <a href="https://www.mohonkpreserve.org/">Mohonk Preserve</a>
            maintain the ground this app describes, and take donations now, on
            their own sites — they deserve them. NY State Parks and NYS DEC
            maintain trails here too, funded through the state rather than a
            donation page. We are not affiliated with any of them — we just
            think your money belongs with the people holding the tools.
          </p>
        </div>
      </div>
    </section>

    <section class="section--tight prose">
      <div class="section__head">
        <span class="blaze"></span>
        <h2>The model, when it arrives</h2>
      </div>
      <p style="margin-bottom: 12px">
        The designed structure is simple to say: optional passes for the
        convenience of long-distance use — a thru-hike pass, regional passes,
        an all-access ceiling — with the proceeds directed to the
        organizations whose trails they came from — the ATC, the maintaining
        clubs, and the agencies who steward public land — and a volunteer
        exemption so the people doing trail work never pay for the map of the
        trail they maintain.
      </p>
      <p style="margin-bottom: 12px">
        None of it is live, no dates are promised, and when it does arrive it
        will be on this page with the accounting alongside it. Payments will
        only ever happen here on the web — never inside the app — so app-store
        fees never take a cut of trail money.
      </p>
      <p>
        Running costs today are small and covered by the volunteers building
        it: the map data is served from commodity storage, the site is static
        hosting, and the design goal is that it stays cheap enough to be
        inherited (<a
          href="https://github.com/OurHike/OurHike/blob/main/OurHikeValues.md"
          >value #7</a
        >).
      </p>
    </section>

    <section class="section--tight prose" style="padding-bottom: 24px">
      <div class="section__head">
        <span class="blaze"></span>
        <h2>Other ways to help</h2>
      </div>
      <p>
        Report what you find on the trail from inside the app — a dry spring, a
        blowdown, a washed-out crossing reaches a moderator who can act on it.
        Or help build the thing itself:
        <a href="https://github.com/OurHike/OurHike">the whole project is open
        source</a>, and the issue tracker is where the work is.
      </p>
    </section>
  </main>
</Base>
